{"data":{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13255","prefix":"10.48550","suffix":"arxiv.2510.13255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.13255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"alternateIdentifiers":[{"alternateIdentifierType":"arXiv","alternateIdentifier":"2510.13255"}],"creators":[{"name":"An, Jingmin","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingmin","familyName":"An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Song, Yilong","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilong","familyName":"Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Yang, Ruolin","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruolin","familyName":"Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Ding, Nai","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nai","familyName":"Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Lu, Lingxi","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingxi","familyName":"Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Wang, Yuxuan","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Wang, Wei","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Zhuang, Chu","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chu","familyName":"Zhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Wang, Qian","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Fang, Fang","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hierarchical Frequency Tagging Probe (HFTP): A Unified Approach to Investigate Syntactic Structure Representations in Large Language Models and the Human Brain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"lang":"en","subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"lang":"en","subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-15T08:04:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:33:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"ris":"GEN","bibtex":"misc","citeproc":"article","schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceType":"Article","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license","rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"}],"descriptions":[{"description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate human-level or even superior language abilities, effectively modeling syntactic structures, yet the specific computational modules responsible remain unclear. A key question is whether LLM behavioral capabilities stem from mechanisms akin to those in the human brain. To address these questions, we introduce the Hierarchical Frequency Tagging Probe (HFTP), a tool that utilizes frequency-domain analysis to identify neuron-wise components of LLMs (e.g., individual Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) neurons) and cortical regions (via intracranial recordings) encoding syntactic structures. Our results show that models such as GPT-2, Gemma, Gemma 2, Llama 2, Llama 3.1, and GLM-4 process syntax in analogous layers, while the human brain relies on distinct cortical regions for different syntactic levels. Representational similarity analysis reveals a stronger alignment between LLM representations and the left hemisphere of the brain (dominant in language processing). Notably, upgraded models exhibit divergent trends: Gemma 2 shows greater brain similarity than Gemma, while Llama 3.1 shows less alignment with the brain compared to Llama 2. These findings offer new insights into the interpretability of LLM behavioral improvements, raising questions about whether these advancements are driven by human-like or non-human-like mechanisms, and establish HFTP as a valuable tool bridging computational linguistics and cognitive neuroscience. This project is available at https://github.com/LilTiger/HFTP.","descriptionType":"Abstract"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"xml":"","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"viewsOverTime":[],"downloadCount":0,"downloadsOverTime":[],"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"citationsOverTime":[],"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-16T01:56:30.000Z","registered":"2025-10-16T01:56:30.000Z","published":"2025","updated":"2025-10-16T01:56:30.000Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}},"provider":{"data":{"id":"arxiv","type":"providers"}},"media":{"data":{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.13255","type":"media"}},"references":{"data":[]},"citations":{"data":[]},"parts":{"data":[]},"partOf":{"data":[]},"versions":{"data":[]},"versionOf":{"data":[]}}}}